# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
# Runtime and state directories for CARLOS EMR.
#
# Declaring them here rather than creating them in postinst means they are also
# recreated after a /run wipe on reboot, and that a mode drift is corrected on
# every boot instead of persisting silently.
#
# LAYOUT RULE, learned the hard way: the two top-level directories are owned by
# ROOT and everything owned by a service account hangs underneath them.
# systemd-tmpfiles refuses to descend from a directory owned by one unprivileged
# user into one owned by a different unprivileged user — it reports
# "Detected unsafe path transition" and SKIPS the entry, which is a
# symlink-attack defence and not something to argue with. With
# /var/lib/carlos-emr owned by `carlos`, the ModSecurity spool (www-data) and
# the ACME webroot (root) below it were silently never created, and nginx then
# failed to start. Root-owned parents make every transition safe.
#
# Type  Path                                      Mode UID           GID   Age Argument
d /run/carlos-emr                                 0750 carlos        carlos -

# --- state ------------------------------------------------------------------
d /var/lib/carlos-emr                             0755 root          root   -
# Tomcat's writable state: the JSP work directory, the servlet temp directory,
# a lib directory a site can drop a JDBC driver or JCE provider into, and the
# java.util.prefs root (a JVM that cannot write one logs a warning per start).
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/catalina                    0750 carlos        carlos -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/catalina/webapps            0750 carlos        carlos -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/catalina/work               0750 carlos        carlos -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/catalina/temp               0750 carlos        carlos -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/catalina/lib                0750 carlos        carlos -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/catalina/prefs              0750 carlos        carlos -
# (The former /var/lib/carlos-emr/browser HOME for an in-JVM-spawned Chromium is
# gone: the render browser now runs under carlos-emr-chromedriver as
# carlos-render with its own /var/lib/carlos-emr/render HOME, owned by that
# package's tmpfiles. The JVM's user.home comes from passwd and never needed it.)
# A heap dump of this application IS a PHI artefact — it contains whatever
# clinical data was in memory. It must never land in /tmp.
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/heapdumps                   0750 carlos        carlos -
# The document store holds scanned patient records. Mode 2750: the application
# (owner) reads and writes, group `carlos` reads — and carlos-backup is a
# member of that group, which is how the backup reaches patient documents
# without being given the application uid. The setgid bit makes every file the
# application creates inherit the group, so the backup keeps working without a
# periodic chmod sweep. Nobody else on the host can even list it.
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/OscarDocument               2750 carlos        carlos -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/OscarDocument/carlos        2750 carlos        carlos -
# ModSecurity's request-body spool and persistent collections, owned by the
# nginx worker uid at 0700: a spooled body is an unfiltered request that may
# contain PHI.
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/modsecurity                 0750 www-data      www-data -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/modsecurity/tmp             0700 www-data      www-data -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/modsecurity/data            0700 www-data      www-data -
# ACME HTTP-01 webroot. Root-owned and world-readable: certbot writes the
# challenge token as root and nginx serves it as www-data. Nothing sensitive is
# ever placed here.
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/acme                        0755 root          root   -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/acme/.well-known            0755 root          root   -
d /var/lib/carlos-emr/acme/.well-known/acme-challenge 0755 root      root   -

# --- logs -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same root-owned-parent rule: the container is root's, and the two writers own
# a subdirectory each. Group adm so the standard log-reading group can follow
# them without being handed either service account.
d /var/log/carlos-emr                             0755 root          root   -
# Tomcat's access and container logs, and the application's own Log4j2 files.
# 2750, not 0750: Tomcat (owner carlos) creates access/JULI logs here, and
# without the setgid bit they inherit group carlos — unreadable by the adm
# group this directory is grouped to for log access. setgid makes new files
# inherit adm so `journalctl`-style log readers in group adm can read them.
d /var/log/carlos-emr/tomcat                      2750 carlos        adm    -
# ModSecurity's audit and debug logs. Named modsec/, not waf/, because the
# application's Log4j2 configuration already writes a waf.log of its own (its
# request rate-limit logger) into the directory above.
# ROOT-owned, not www-data-owned, even though nginx is the writer. With
# SecAuditLogType Serial the audit log is opened once by the nginx MASTER
# process — which is root — and the workers write through the inherited
# descriptor; no www-data process ever needs to create a file here. Making the
# directory root-owned lets the `z` rules below re-assert the log's mode at
# boot: systemd-tmpfiles refuses to descend from a www-data-owned directory
# into a root-owned file ("unsafe path transition") and would skip them.
d /var/log/carlos-emr/modsec                      0750 root          adm    -
# libmodsecurity's SecAuditLogFileMode is not honoured for Serial logs, so the
# file lands 0644 under the master's umask. The 0750 directory above is what
# actually denies everyone outside group adm — verified by reading it as
# `nobody` — but re-assert the file's own mode too rather than leave a
# PHI-adjacent log world-readable in principle. `z` only acts on a path that
# already exists, so these are no-ops on a fresh system.
z /var/log/carlos-emr/modsec/modsec_audit.log      0640 root          adm    -
z /var/log/carlos-emr/modsec/modsec_debug.log      0640 root          adm    -

# --- backups ----------------------------------------------------------------
# 0700 and owned by carlos-backup: the application uid must not be able to
# read, alter or delete the backups it would be recovered from.
d /var/backups/carlos-emr                         0700 carlos-backup carlos-backup -
# Root-filesystem copy of the backup repository marker: the copy inside
# /var/backups sits on the (possibly dedicated, possibly unmounted) backup
# volume, and an unmounted volume must not erase the evidence that a
# repository existed there.
d /var/log/carlos-emr/backup-state                0700 carlos-backup adm    -
# A crash defeats the backup's EXIT trap; whatever plaintext dump it left in
# the work directory is removed at boot (the script also purges at run start).
R! /var/backups/carlos-emr/work

# --- TLS --------------------------------------------------------------------
# The private key is 0640 root:www-data. nginx reads it as root before dropping
# privileges; the application uid has no path to it at all.
d /etc/carlos-emr/tls                             0750 root          www-data -
